{
  "requirement": "`habeas::validate_repository(root)` MUST return a report with the number of discovered requirements, the number of evidence files, and all validation errors for the supplied project. Ordinary invalid evidence MUST be reported in that report; an inaccessible project root MUST return an I/O error.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is implemented and tested, with two minor caveats noted below.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `validate_repository` (src/evidence.rs:119) is the public entry point. It is re-exported from `lib.rs`, and it returns `inspect_repository(root)?.report`.\n- `inspect_repository_with_progress` (src/evidence.rs:172-301) starts with `fs::canonicalize(root)?`. A missing or inaccessible root therefore returns an `io::Error` and never produces a report.\n- Once the root is accessible, every validation problem is pushed into `errors` instead of being returned as an error. This covers:\n  - workspace config errors\n  - book source errors\n  - definition errors\n  - evidence file parse and schema errors\n  - orphaned evidence\n  - missing evidence\n  - reference coverage\n  - Rust item coverage\n\n  Ordinary invalid evidence is therefore reported, as the requirement asks.\n- `EvidenceReport` (src/evidence.rs:16-23) carries:\n  - `requirements`, counting only `DefinitionKind::Requirement`, so terms and examples are excluded\n  - `evidence_files`, the number of JSON files found under `evidence/`\n  - `errors`, the full list of validation errors\n- All of these are marked `{impl}` for this requirement.\n\n**Caveats**\n- The report also carries an `examples` count that the requirement does not mention. It is harmless, it is used in the `validate()` failure message, and it is plausibly part of the examples feature. I treated it as a non-gap.\n- Only a root that cannot be canonicalized (for example, one that does not exist) is guaranteed to give an I/O error. A root that exists but cannot be read might surface as validation errors instead. The requirement does not define \"inaccessible\" more precisely, so I did not count this as a gap.\n\n**Tests**\n- `inaccessible_project_root_is_an_io_error_not_a_report` (tests/evidence_boundaries.rs:557) checks the failure case. A missing root returns `ErrorKind::NotFound`. In an accessible root, a broken `evidence/unknown.thing.json` appears in `report.errors` instead of causing an `Err`.\n- `validates_full_names_requirement_text_and_input_lines` checks `requirements == 1`, `evidence_files == 1` and empty errors on valid evidence.\n- `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` is a broad test that all error kinds are collected in one report. It covers:\n  - missing evidence\n  - orphan evidence\n  - invalid JSON\n  - schema errors\n  - a false determination\n  - changed text\n  - changed outline hashes\n  - duplicate and empty definitions\n- `inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase` compares the report counts and errors across valid and invalid states.\n- Other tests also call `validate_repository` on realistic fixtures:\n  - the example test in src/evidence.rs, which asserts requirement, example and evidence-file counts\n  - the external Cargo output test\n  - the FIXME fixture in tests/commands.rs\n\nTogether these cover the counts, aggregation of all errors, ordinary invalid evidence being reported, and the I/O error case.",
  "references": "b29e530bf260b1400a1b11fe3542a589c48af5d5952ad5be6bfe6199326d9394",
